<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.ViewModels"
             mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="268" d:DesignHeight="520"
             x:Class="KinesisEdit.Views.TapAndHoldPanelView"
             x:DataType="vm:TapAndHoldPanelViewModel">
  <!--
    The key inspector's Tap & hold panel (mockup 2h) — specs/11-feature-dialogs.md §11.1 in the
    268px rail instead of a 520px modal.

    NEITHER ACTION FIELD IS A TextBox, AND THAT IS THE POINT. Focus inside a TextBox auto-suspends
    the keystroke-capture service, so a focusable field would swallow the very keypress it exists to
    record. They are Borders on the `TokenField` control theme (Classes="actionField"), which
    declares its own `.armed` face — the amber "press something now" border a listening key wears.

    THE PANEL REFUSES POLITELY RATHER THAN DISAPPEARING. Everywhere else in the app a feature the
    device lacks is not drawn at all; a firmware gate (09 §2) and the four pre-dialog checks of
    §11.1 are the sanctioned exception, because the user pointed at this tab and a tab that vanished
    would be a question with no answer. The refusal is amber — the same ramp the gate's own message
    box uses (MessageBoxIcon.Warning) — and it is never red: nothing here failed.

    EVERY LABEL BUT TWO IS SANS. Mono is reserved for values that exist verbatim in a config file:
    the two action tokens and the `250 ms` readout are, and `Tap — a quick press sends`,
    `default 250 · this device` and the capture rule are the app talking about them.

    THE RECORD DOT IS GEOMETRY, NOT A GLYPH. Mockup 2h writes the buttons as `● Record`, but U+25CF
    is in NEITHER embedded IBM Plex family — it would draw as tofu, exactly the failure
    ShapeAndTypeTokenTests holds every keycap legend to. The dot is an Ellipse on the error ramp
    (red is the record colour in the mock) and the caption is the word alone.
  -->
  <StackPanel>

    <!-- ===== What the profile already costs ==================================================
         Drawn ABOVE both faces and outside the availability gate, because it is true either way —
         and because it is frequently the *explanation* for the refusal below it: a profile at 11
         of 10 refuses every further assignment through §11.1's second pre-dialog check, and the
         count is the answer to "why".

         Amber and non-blocking, read off the editor's advisory set and never recomputed or
         re-worded here. The layout still saves; the board keeps the first ten lines it reads. -->
    <Border Classes="inspectorSection" IsVisible="{Binding HasBudgetAdvisory}">
      <Border Background="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBrush}"
              BorderBrush="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBorderBrush}"
              BorderThickness="1"
              CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}"
              Padding="9,7">
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding BudgetAdvisory}"
                   Classes="body statusWarning"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap" />
      </Border>
    </Border>

    <!-- ===== The refusal ======================================================================
         Drawn INSTEAD of the fields, never beside them: a panel that showed both would be offering
         an edit it has just said it cannot make.

         No heading. §11.1's `Assign Tap and Hold Action` is a DIALOG title and this is not a
         dialog — the rail's own `Tap & hold` tab, directly above, already names the panel, and
         repeating it here said the same thing twice in a 268px column.

         `Update Firmware` appears only for the FIRMWARE refusal, and only where the device has a
         support page. A profile that has spent its ten tap-and-holds refuses too, and offering a
         firmware update there would answer a question nobody asked. -->
    <Border Classes="inspectorSection" IsVisible="{Binding !IsAvailable}">
      <StackPanel Spacing="8">
        <Border Background="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBrush}"
                BorderBrush="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBorderBrush}"
                BorderThickness="1"
                CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}"
                Padding="9,7">
          <TextBlock Text="{Binding UnavailableReason}"
                     Classes="body statusWarning"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap" />
        </Border>

        <Button Classes="secondary"
                Content="{Binding UpdateFirmwareCaption}"
                Command="{Binding UpdateFirmwareCommand}"
                IsVisible="{Binding CanUpdateFirmware}"
                HorizontalAlignment="Left" />
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>

    <!-- ===== The two captures ================================================================= -->
    <StackPanel IsVisible="{Binding IsAvailable}">
      <!-- Hidden while §11.6's catalog is up: the pick is one question with one answer, and 244px
           of rail cannot hold a grouped list under two fields and a slider. -->
      <StackPanel IsVisible="{Binding !IsPickerOpen}">
        <Border Classes="inspectorSection">
          <StackPanel Spacing="8">

            <!-- Tap. A Grid rather than a horizontal StackPanel: a StackPanel measures its children
                 with infinite width, so the field would never give the button room and a long token
                 would run off the 268px rail.

                 §11.1's `Search` sits on the label line rather than beside the Record button: it is
                 the second way to fill the same field, and a third control on the field row would
                 leave the token itself too narrow to read. -->
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="6">
                <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                           Text="{x:Static vm:TapAndHoldPanelViewModel.TapFieldLabel}"
                           Classes="body"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                <Button Grid.Column="1"
                        Classes="link"
                        Content="{x:Static vm:TapAndHoldPanelViewModel.SearchCaption}"
                        ToolTip.Tip="{x:Static vm:TapAndHoldPanelViewModel.SearchHint}"
                        Command="{Binding SearchTapActionCommand}"
                        VerticalAlignment="Top" />
              </Grid>
              <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
                <Border Grid.Column="0"
                        Classes="actionField"
                        ToolTip.Tip="{x:Static vm:TapAndHoldPanelViewModel.TapActionHint}"
                        Classes.armed="{Binding ArmedField, Converter={StaticResource EnumMatch}, ConverterParameter=Tap}">
                  <TextBlock Text="{Binding TapActionText}"
                             Classes="monoValue"
                             TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                </Border>
                <Button Grid.Column="1"
                        Classes="recordAction"
                        Command="{Binding ArmTapActionCommand}">
                  <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <Ellipse Classes="recordDot" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    <!-- The 1.4s pulse (DurationRecordingPulse), reached by the class the listening
                         cap and the recording macro banner already share — one looping animation in
                         the whole app, and the budget allows exactly one. Bound rather than fixed, so
                         it runs only while THIS field is the armed one. -->
                    <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:TapAndHoldPanelViewModel.RecordCaption}"
                               Classes.recording="{Binding ArmedField, Converter={StaticResource EnumMatch}, ConverterParameter=Tap}"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                  </StackPanel>
                </Button>
              </Grid>
            </StackPanel>

            <!-- Hold. Identical shape; the label is what says the two are different captures. -->
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
              <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="6">
                <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                           Text="{x:Static vm:TapAndHoldPanelViewModel.HoldFieldLabel}"
                           Classes="body"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                <Button Grid.Column="1"
                        Classes="link"
                        Content="{x:Static vm:TapAndHoldPanelViewModel.SearchCaption}"
                        ToolTip.Tip="{x:Static vm:TapAndHoldPanelViewModel.SearchHint}"
                        Command="{Binding SearchHoldActionCommand}"
                        VerticalAlignment="Top" />
              </Grid>
              <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
                <Border Grid.Column="0"
                        Classes="actionField"
                        ToolTip.Tip="{x:Static vm:TapAndHoldPanelViewModel.HoldActionHint}"
                        Classes.armed="{Binding ArmedField, Converter={StaticResource EnumMatch}, ConverterParameter=Hold}">
                  <TextBlock Text="{Binding HoldActionText}"
                             Classes="monoValue"
                             TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                </Border>
                <Button Grid.Column="1"
                        Classes="recordAction"
                        Command="{Binding ArmHoldActionCommand}">
                  <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <Ellipse Classes="recordDot" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:TapAndHoldPanelViewModel.RecordCaption}"
                               Classes.recording="{Binding ArmedField, Converter={StaticResource EnumMatch}, ConverterParameter=Hold}"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                  </StackPanel>
                </Button>
              </Grid>
            </StackPanel>

            <!-- Why the two are captured separately at all, verbatim from mockup 2h. -->
            <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:TapAndHoldPanelViewModel.CaptureRule}"
                       Classes="meta muted"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap" />
          </StackPanel>
        </Border>

        <!-- ===== The delay ======================================================================
             1-999 ms, opened on the device's own default (250 ms; 150 on the Advantage 360) — read
             from TapAndHoldCapability, never a literal here.

             KNOWN GAP: the slider keeps Fluent's 50px vertical extent. Tightening it means rewriting
             `Grid#HorizontalTemplate`, which needs the template-part names the control-theme layer
             refuses to hand out (Themes/ControlThemes/Fields.axaml, docs/app/design-system.md). -->
        <Border Classes="inspectorSection">
          <StackPanel Spacing="4">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                         Text="{x:Static vm:TapAndHoldPanelViewModel.DelayLabel}"
                         Classes="body"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <!-- Mono: `250` is literally what `[t&h250]` carries in the layout file. -->
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                         Text="{Binding DelayReadout}"
                         Classes="monoValue"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Grid>

            <!-- Bound to DelaySliderValue, not to DelayMilliseconds: a Slider coerces its Value into
                 its own range and writes the coerced number back, which would silently rewrite an
                 out-of-range delay from an older firmware's file the instant this panel drew it. -->
            <Slider Minimum="{Binding MinimumDelayMilliseconds}"
                    Maximum="{Binding MaximumDelayMilliseconds}"
                    Value="{Binding DelaySliderValue}"
                    TickFrequency="1"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True"
                    ToolTip.Tip="{x:Static vm:TapAndHoldPanelViewModel.DelayHint}" />

            <TextBlock Text="{Binding DelayDefaultCaption}"
                       Classes="meta muted"
                       IsVisible="{Binding HasDelayDefaultCaption}"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap" />

            <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:TapAndHoldPanelViewModel.NoteText}"
                       Classes="meta muted"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap" />
          </StackPanel>
        </Border>

        <!-- ===== The write ======================================================================
             No bottom hairline: this is the last block of the panel, and the rail's footer draws its
             own line above itself. -->
        <Border Classes="inspectorSection" BorderThickness="0">
          <StackPanel Spacing="8">

            <!-- §11.1's three validation messages and the locked-position refusal. Red, because this
                 one really is a refusal to write rather than a note about a legal layout. -->
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding ValidationMessage}"
                       Classes="body statusError"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       IsVisible="{Binding HasValidationMessage}" />

            <Button Classes="primaryAction"
                    Content="{x:Static vm:TapAndHoldPanelViewModel.AssignCaption}"
                    Command="{Binding AssignCommand}"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Left" />
          </StackPanel>
        </Border>
      </StackPanel>

      <!-- ===== §11.6's catalog, INSIDE the panel ================================================
           The overlay this replaced nested a second modal over the first, which is the only reason
           EditorOverlayHost ever had a ShowNested. The rail nests nothing: this is the very same
           TokenPickerViewModel the Remap panel hosts, shown in place of the fields until a row is
           taken. It is what keeps a media key, a mouse button or a profile selector — none of which
           a keyboard can press — assignable as a tap or a hold action. -->
      <Border Classes="inspectorSection" BorderThickness="0" IsVisible="{Binding IsPickerOpen}">
        <StackPanel Spacing="8">
          <!-- Which of the two fields this pick fills, in that field's own words. -->
          <TextBlock Text="{Binding PickerFieldLabel}" Classes="body" TextWrapping="Wrap" />

          <ContentControl Content="{Binding Picker}" />

          <Button Classes="secondary"
                  Content="{x:Static vm:TapAndHoldPanelViewModel.CancelSearchCaption}"
                  Command="{Binding CloseSearchCommand}"
                  HorizontalAlignment="Left" />
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>
    </StackPanel>
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
